# No Silent Known Bugs

**Precedence:** standing user instruction.

## Intent

Help write correct code. If while hunting, fixing, or reviewing you observe something that is **actually wrong**, do not walk past it — whether or not it matches the issue you were hired to fix.

## Rule

Any **confirmed or strongly suspected defect** you observe while working must be classified — **name the bucket**. Do not use bare “follow-up?” (overloaded; agents escape Done with it). Full leftover taxonomy: [github-issue-workflow.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/github-issue-workflow.mdc).

1. **In scope** — required for *this* issue to be done right. Expand acceptance (comment + checkboxes if needed); finish on the **same** `wip/<issue-slug>` branch. Parent is blocked until finished.
2. **Child / sub-issue** — still part of a parent/epic. Open a child issue; link from the parent; parent acceptance may checkbox the child. Parent **stays open** until the child is fully done/integrated (or parent acceptance no longer requires it). A child is **not** an escape hatch.
3. **Follow-up** — optional / truly out of this issue’s Done (“you might also…”, similar pattern elsewhere). **Own** open GitHub issue per item, created immediately; one-line discovery comment with the number. Parent may close after land **only if** no in-scope or required-child work remains.

**Do not silently ignore it.** “Out of scope / don’t expand / leave it / unrelated, skip” without naming the bucket and tracking it (same issue, child, or own follow-up issue) is forbidden. Closed issues, PR summaries, and chat are not a backlog.

**Related ≠ found-while.** Related means the *work* relates (same defect class, dependency, or an explicit do-not-absorb split). It does not mean you noticed B while doing A. Changing the oil and seeing a low brake pad: file the pad; do not mark it related to the oil. A discovery comment with the new number is the trail; the issue body Related/see-also line is only for actual relation.

**Batch related work, not found-while.** Related issues may be picked up together (one joint worktree / `wip/` branch) when that is cheaper than two trips — same types, same tests, same breaking change. Found-while issues stay their own tickets and their own trips unless you explicitly expand scope. Filing first is still required; batching is scheduling, not silence.

Uncertain is not ignore: state confidence (“I think…”, “unverified”) and still put it on the thread or an issue.

Do not drive-by-fix unless it is explicitly in this issue’s scope.

## Defect vs nit

**Defect** — correctness, identity/lifecycle, security, broken contracts, missing tests for claimed behavior, adjacent logic bugs on the path you are in. Name the site (type/file/call) and what fails or can fail.

**Nit** — style, naming taste, speculative refactors, “I would have written it differently.” Nits are not this rule. Do not file them.

Similarity is irrelevant. Same class, similar, or unrelated-but-wrong all follow the fork above (name the bucket).

## Example

- **In scope:** collapse keyed on ephemeral `section.id` stays *in* #474 (same identity bug as settings `id = UUID()`).
- **Follow-up (similar pattern elsewhere):** #475 other catalog types with the same `id = UUID()` pattern — split so #474 stays settings-only; own issue; #474 may close without waiting on #475.
- **Follow-up (found-while, not related):** #476 stale `@State` on catalog rebuild, #477 unused `PresentationHints` — filed because they were visible under `GenericSettingsView`, not because they are the UUID bug. Do not bundle them into a 474/475 trip.
